{# ============================================================================
   THE SOURCE RECORD, BESIDE THE FORM — the rail's first card.

   The thin strip at the top of the page proves the observation exists; it cannot
   be READ. Here the same record is legible: its own words, where it was recorded,
   its photographs at a size that shows what is in them, and the record itself —
   which patrol, whose eyes, when, where.

   NOTHING ON IT IS EDITABLE. Provenance is written once, at filing, and never
   again; there is not a control on this card and the seam test holds it to that.

   IT RENDERS ONLY WHERE THE FILING CAME FROM A RECORD. A walk-in filing has
   nothing to show, and the rail is the checklist alone.

   EVERY ROW IS CONDITIONAL, because the hand-off is a query string another module
   composes: a module with no patrol, no named observer or no coordinates sends
   what it has, and a row nobody was told about is absent rather than empty.

   Parameters: prefill, files, map
   ========================================================================== #}
<div class="c" data-incident-observation>
    <span class="tab">The observation<span class="src">· {{ prefill.label }} · not editable</span></span>

    {# THE OBSERVATION'S OWN WORDS, quoted — so it reads as somebody else's
       sentence rather than as this form's prefilled answer, which is the field
       below it and is editable. #}
    {% if prefill.note %}<p class="rr-quote">“{{ prefill.note }}”</p>{% endif %}

    {# WHERE IT WAS RECORDED. The atlas's plate — the deployment's imagery, the
       one boundary treatment, the control stack top-right and fullscreen — told
       its height and nothing else, because here it answers one question rather
       than being the page's subject.

       The height is a custom property on the PLATE, which is what the atlas
       documents: handed to render_map() it sizes the plate, and set on the canvas
       inside it would size nothing. It is the ONE thing a module may say about a
       plate — the imagery, the control stack, the frame, fullscreen and where the
       legend goes are the atlas's, so that a map reads the same on every screen in
       the product.

       THE LEGEND IS THE ONE UNFINISHED PIECE, and it is not this module's to fix:
       the atlas floats it over the imagery, which is right on a plate that is the
       page's subject and wrong on a 176px one. See docs/design-decisions.md. #}
    {% if map %}
        <span class="rr-sub">Where it was recorded</span>
        {{ render_map(map, {
            '--map-plate-height': '176px',
            'role': 'img',
            'aria-label': 'Where ' ~ prefill.label ~ ' was recorded',
        }) }}
    {% endif %}

    {# THE PHOTOGRAPHS, AT THE RAIL'S WIDTH. The strip above the form draws them
       76px wide, which is enough to prove a photograph exists and not enough to
       read one. They arrive through the platform's file registry, which asks the
       module that OWNS the record — this bundle knows a token and a uuid and
       nothing else — and they open in the shared preview, the one component every
       surface opens a file in.

       A BUTTON, NOT A LINK: the preview component reads a click inside an <a> as
       navigation and stands aside, so a thumbnail wrapped in one would leave the
       flow for a raw image file. `type=button` is stated even though the rail sits
       outside the form: a button's default is submit, and a control that would
       submit something is not a control anybody should have to reason about. #}
    {% if files is not empty %}
        {% import '@UhifadhiStorage/overlay/_preview.html.twig' as preview %}
        <span class="rr-sub">Its {{ files|length }} photograph{{ 1 == files|length ? '' : 's' }}</span>
        <div class="rr-shots">
            {% for file in files %}
                <button type="button" class="rr-shot"
                        title="{{ file.name }}{% if file.takenAt %} · {{ file.takenAt|date('H:i') }}{% endif %}"
                        {{ preview.entry(file, '') }}>
                    <img src="{{ path('storage_evidence_show', {key: file.thumbKey ?? file.key}) }}"
                         alt="{{ file.name }}, from {{ prefill.label }}" loading="lazy">
                    <i>{{ file.name }}{% if file.takenAt %} · {{ file.takenAt|date('H:i', false) }}{% endif %}</i>
                </button>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
        {{ include('@UhifadhiStorage/overlay/_preview.html.twig') }}
    {% endif %}

    <span class="rr-sub">The record itself</span>
    {% if prefill.patrol %}<div class="rln"><span>Patrol</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.patrol }}</span></div>{% endif %}
    {% if prefill.ranger %}<div class="rln"><span>Recorded by</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.ranger }}</span></div>{% endif %}
    {# `false` KEEPS THE MOMENT'S OWN ZONE. The observation was made at 08:15 where
       the observer was standing, and a record meant to be recognised must not
       restate it in UTC. #}
    {% if prefill.occurredAt %}
        <div class="rln"><span>When</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.occurredAt|date('D j M Y · H:i', false)|lower }}</span></div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if prefill.positionLabel %}
        <div class="rln"><span>Where</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.positionLabel }}</span></div>
    {% endif %}
    <div class="rln"><span>Source</span><span class="mono">{{ prefill.sourceLabel }}</span></div>

    <div class="use">The filer is describing something they may not have seen. Everything the person who did see it
        wrote down is one glance to the right, at a size that can be read, for as long as the form is open.</div>
</div>
